---
import CallToAction from '../components/CallToAction.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const stats = [
	{ big: '~3 : 1', text: 'ratio of men to women on the most popular apps' },
	{ big: '~2%', text: "of a typical man's right-swipes become a match - 50 swipes to get one match!" },
	{ big: '30k', text: 'profiles a typical man may swipe to find one relationship' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Swiper, No Swiping | Marcus Levine"
	description="Fed up with dating apps? They're addictive by design, plus the odds are stacked against you. Why not play a different game?"
>
	<div class="stack gap-20">
		<main class="wrapper stack gap-15 swiper">
			<Hero
				title="Swiper, No Swiping."
				tagline="For those who are sick of dating apps."
			>
				<div class="hero-cta">
					<CallToAction href="/webinar/">
						Join the free webinar
						<Icon icon="arrow-right" size="1.2em" />
					</CallToAction>
					<p class="cta-note">Swiped Out: How Dating Apps Keep You Single & What Actually Works · 45 minutes, live</p>
				</div>
			</Hero>

			<section class="content stack gap-4">
				<p>
					You've probably felt it: weeks of swiping, barely a match. 
					The apps are brutal.
				</p>
				<p>
					What the apps won't tell you: <strong>the deck is stacked.</strong> These platforms make
					money when you keep swiping — not when you fall in love and delete them. The numbers
					for the average guy are especially rough:
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="stats">
				{
					stats.map(({ big, text }) => (
						<div class="stat">
							<div class="stat-big">{big}</div>
							<p>{text}</p>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</section>

			<section class="content stack gap-4">
				<h2>It feels like a slot machine</h2>
				<p>
					Variable rewards, an endless feed, and an invisible ranking system deciding who even
					sees you. Men swipe right on about 53% of profiles while women swipe right on roughly
					12% — so a small number of profiles soak up most of the attention from women. 
					Add a scoreboard that buries "desperate" swiping, and the average guy doesn't 
					stand a chance.
				</p>
				<h2>…and it takes your money, too</h2>
				<p>
					Men are more likely to pay for premium and, because matches are scarcer, get far less for
					it. On the same subscription, men can pay 1.5× to 4.5× more per date than women. One
					analysis pegged the cost to land a single date on a premium plan at about $56 for men
					vs. $10 for women. You pay more for worse odds.
				</p>
				<p class="pull">
					None of this means you're undesirable. The apps are designed to keep you swiping.
				</p>
				<p class="fine">
					<strong>An honest caveat:</strong> plenty of good relationships start on apps — maybe
					yours will. I'm not saying the apps are evil. I'm just pointing out that the odds are
					terrible for men — and that there's another game most people have stopped playing.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="content stack gap-4">
				<h2>The better odds are in real life</h2>
				<p>
					Think about the math for a second. On the apps you're one of thousands of profiles,
					ranked and filtered, competing on a photo. In a café, a bookshop, a dance floor, a
					queue — you're a real human being, politely but directly approaching another human
					being you're attracted to. Face-to-face, eye-to-eye.
				</p>
				<p>That is a completely different game, and the odds are far better.</p>
				<h3>For the stubborn swiper — one tip</h3>
				<p>
					<strong>Swipe like a sniper, not a shotgun.</strong> The single most powerful free lever
					in the data: men who right-swipe on under 4% of profiles match at about 11.9% — versus
					2.2% for the guys who swipe right on everyone. That's a 5.4× improvement from
					selectivity alone. Spray-swiping tells the algorithm you're low-value and hides you. So
					slow down, be picky. Oh, and put some real effort into your first photo.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="content stack gap-4">
				<h2>The reframe</h2>
				<p>
					The freeze you feel before saying hi isn't a character flaw — it's an untrained nervous
					system. It's not in your head; it's in your body. So that's where we train it: calm the
					response, and let rejection become practice instead of defeat.
				</p>
				<p>
					No pickup lines. No creepiness. Just the training you need to walk up and start a
					conversation with someone you're attracted to.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="final-cta stack gap-4">
				<h2>Ready to stop swiping?</h2>
				<p>
					I go deeper on all of this — the numbers, how your body reacts, and the one honest
					question to connect without cornering — in a free 45-minute live session:
					<strong>Swiped Out: How Dating Apps Keep You Single & What Actually Works.</strong>
				</p>
				<div class="hero-cta">
					<CallToAction href="/webinar/">
						Save my spot
						<Icon icon="arrow-right" size="1.2em" />
					</CallToAction>
					<p class="cta-note">Let's bring back in-person dating!</p>
				</div>
			</section>
		</main>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.swiper {
		padding-top: 2rem;
	}

	.hero-cta {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		margin-top: 1rem;
	}

	.cta-note {
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		color: var(--gray-400);
		max-width: 40ch;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.content {
		max-width: 65ch;
		margin-inline: auto;
		width: 100%;
		font-size: var(--text-md);
		color: var(--gray-200);
	}

	.content h2,
	.content h3 {
		color: var(--gray-0);
	}

	.content h2 {
		font-size: var(--text-2xl);
	}

	.content h3 {
		font-size: var(--text-xl);
	}

	.fine {
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		color: var(--gray-400);
	}

	.stats {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(12rem, 1fr));
		gap: 1rem;
		max-width: 65rem;
		margin-inline: auto;
		width: 100%;
	}

	.stat {
		background: var(--gray-999_40);
		border: 1px solid var(--gray-800);
		border-radius: 1.5rem;
		padding: 1.5rem;
		text-align: center;
		box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
	}

	.stat-big {
		font-family: var(--font-brand);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: var(--text-3xl);
		color: var(--accent-regular);
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
	}

	.stat p {
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		color: var(--gray-300);
	}

	.final-cta {
		text-align: center;
		align-items: center;
		max-width: 65ch;
		margin-inline: auto;
	}

	.final-cta h2 {
		color: var(--gray-0);
	}
</style>
